ANSWERS = [
    "<code>(hd1,1)</code> counts from 0, so it is the second partition on the second disk, <code>/dev/sdb2</code>. Keep that disk in the second bay so the BIOS still sees it as the second drive.",
    "Press Esc to show the menu if it's hidden, select the entry, press <code>a</code>, type <code>single</code> at the end of the kernel line, and press Enter. (Or <code>e</code>, select the kernel line, <code>e</code>, add it, Enter, then <code>b</code>.) It lasts for that boot only.",
    "<code>grep -E '^GRUB_TIMEOUT' /etc/default/grub /etc/default/grub.d/*.cfg</code> shows <code>GRUB_TIMEOUT_STYLE=hidden</code> or <code>GRUB_TIMEOUT=0</code>. On a copy, set <code>GRUB_TIMEOUT_STYLE=menu</code> and <code>GRUB_TIMEOUT=10</code> and save <code>diff -u</code>; the real change is followed by <code>sudo update-grub</code>.",
    "grub.cfg is regenerated, so hand edits are lost. Put the entry in <code>/etc/grub.d/40_custom</code>, check it with <code>grub-script-check</code>, then run <code>sudo update-grub</code>.",
    "<code>sudo grub-mkconfig &gt; preview.cfg</code> (no <code>-o</code>, so nothing is written to /boot), then <code>sudo diff /boot/grub/grub.cfg preview.cfg</code>.",
    "Add <code>systemd.unit=emergency.target</code> (or <code>emergency</code>) to the linux line at the GRUB menu. It mounts only root, read-only, so the damaged filesystem can be checked.",
    "Set a GRUB2 superuser: generate a hash with <code>grub-mkpasswd-pbkdf2</code>, add <code>set superusers=\"admin\"</code> and <code>password_pbkdf2 admin grub.pbkdf2.sha512…</code> to <code>/etc/grub.d/40_custom</code>, check it with <code>grub-script-check</code>, then run <code>sudo update-grub</code>.",
]
